<main>
<section class="hero-dark page-hero"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Optometry operations guide</span><h1>Build a patient recall workflow that does not depend on memory.</h1><p>Recall is where scheduling, patient history, communication and follow-up meet. A connected workflow makes it easier to identify who is due, contact them consistently and see what happened next.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn-grad" href="https://taxrescrm.app/book?product=oculivo">Request a private demo&nbsp; →</a><a class="btn-outline" href="/optometry-software/">Explore optometry workflows</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section-light"><div class="wrap article-wrap">
<div class="quick"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Recall should be driven by the patient's actual care history, exam cadence and practice protocol—not a disconnected spreadsheet. Each due date should remain attached to the patient and the reason for recall.</div>
<section class="article-section"><h2>Start with recall rules tied to the patient record</h2><p>Recall should be driven by the patient's actual care history, exam cadence and practice protocol—not a disconnected spreadsheet. Each due date should remain attached to the patient and the reason for recall.</p><ul><li>Routine eye exam</li><li>Contact lens follow-up</li><li>Medical follow-up</li><li>Optical pickup or remake</li></ul></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Segment before you message</h2><p>A patient due for an annual comprehensive exam should not receive the same message as someone who missed a contact lens check. Segmenting by recall reason allows the practice to use clearer language and measure which workflows are actually producing appointments.</p></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Track attempts and outcomes</h2><p>The useful question is not only whether a reminder was sent. The practice should know whether the patient booked, asked to be contacted later, declined, transferred care or never responded. That outcome history prevents repetitive outreach and makes reactivation measurable.</p></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Keep recall connected to scheduling capacity</h2><p>Recall campaigns work better when the front desk can see open appointment capacity and route responses directly into available times. The workflow should reduce back-and-forth instead of creating another callback queue.</p></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Measure reactivation, not message volume</h2><p>A successful recall system should help answer how many due patients were contacted, how many booked, how long they had been overdue and which reminder sequences performed best.</p></section>
<section class="faq-block"><span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><article><h3>What is patient recall in optometry?</h3><p>Patient recall is the process of identifying patients due for routine or follow-up care and contacting them to return to the practice.</p></article><article><h3>How often should optometry patients be recalled?</h3><p>Recall cadence depends on clinical need, patient age, risk factors, contact lens use and practice protocol. The system should support the practice's rules rather than assume one interval for everyone.</p></article><article><h3>What should recall software track?</h3><p>Due reason, due date, communication attempts, response, booked appointment, opt-out or transfer status, and reactivation reporting.</p></article></section>
</div></section>
